WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.050
You walk into a room and the conversation suddenly stops; everyone knows how that moment feels.

2
00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:09.860
But what if you were part of the conversation that just stopped?

3
00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:17.510
In this episode, we ask why one of our oldest moral prohibitions may be aimed at the very thing that first held humanity together.

4
00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:30.000
You walk into a room, and all at once the talking stops.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.850
Maybe it's the tea corner of an office, maybe a family dinner table, maybe a little circle of three in a school hallway.

6
00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:43.700
Someone reaches for their glass, someone glances at their phone, and someone smiles just a moment too late.

7
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:49.910
Nobody says a word, but you all know: before you arrived, someone was being talked about here.

8
00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:51.800
Maybe it was you.

9
00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:55.610
Everyone knows that cold feeling in the pit of the stomach.

10
00:00:55.610 --> 00:01:00.860
The strange thing is, the same people also know the warmth of being inside that circle.

11
00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:10.430
Someone lowers their voice, looks around, and begins, "Don't tell anyone, but..." With those words, the listeners can't help leaning in.

12
00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:14.080
Their pupils widen, the coffee goes cold.

13
00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:18.530
For those few minutes, everyone in the circle shares a secret.

14
00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:22.660
They are a little closer to one another than to anyone left outside.

15
00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:25.670
Gossip comes with these two faces.

16
00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:31.880
On one side is the fear of being shut out; on the other, the pleasure of being on the inside.

17
00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:40.810
And this is where the real question begins: if nearly every moral tradition condemns talking behind people's backs, why do we find it so hard to give up?

18
00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:48.940
Why does someone else's divorce, promotion, dressing-down, or secret debt seem more gripping than the important matters in our own lives?

19
00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:51.470
The words themselves offer a clue.

20
00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:59.120
The English word "gossip" comes from an Old English term meaning "related before God," a word used for godparents.

21
00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:05.170
In the Middle Ages, it described the close women friends who waited at a mother's bedside during childbirth.

22
00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:10.260
Over time, the talk that filled those long hours of waiting changed the meaning of the word.

23
00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:15.990
The sense of "close friend" faded into the background, and the sense of "idle talk" came to the fore.

24
00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:20.120
So from the very beginning, gossip was bound up with intimacy.

25
00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:23.450
You only share a secret with someone you trust.

26
00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:29.020
And gossip is, in a way, the shadowed side of that trust.

27
00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:34.110
The Arabic word "gıybet," which passed into Turkish, points to something else.

28
00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:38.080
Its root is "gayb," meaning absence, invisibility.

29
00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:41.410
Gıybet is talk about someone who isn't there.

30
00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:46.500
The "behind their back" of our title is already hidden inside the word.

31
00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:52.390
The person being discussed is not present; they cannot defend themselves or set the story straight.

32
00:02:52.390 --> 00:03:00.520
Perhaps part of the sweetness comes from this: a story told without anyone objecting is always neater, sharper, and more entertaining.

33
00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:04.810
And there is another taste, one that everyone finds hard to admit.

34
00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:15.340
In a brain imaging study published in the journal Science in two thousand nine, Japanese researchers had participants read stories about fictional people who seemed more successful than themselves.

35
00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:21.390
As envy grew, the brain regions associated with pain and conflict became more active.

36
00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:29.040
Then, when participants read that misfortune had befallen those same people, it was the regions linked to the reward system that lit up.

37
00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:38.930
Schadenfreude, a German word that has spread around the world, meaning the secret joy we take in someone else's misfortune, was leaving its trace in the laboratory too.

38
00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:42.420
Part of gossip feeds on exactly this.

39
00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:45.590
A line like "Did you hear?

40
00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:52.440
That know-it-all finally fell flat on his face" closes, for a moment, a wound inside the listener.

41
00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:56.570
But explaining gossip only as envy or malice would be too easy.

42
00:03:56.570 --> 00:03:59.740
Because even the best-intentioned people gossip.

43
00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:08.670
Someone starting a new job will ask, in their first week, who trusts whom, which manager is short-tempered in the mornings, whose word can be relied on.

44
00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:12.000
Nobody explains these things in an official meeting.

45
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:17.250
That knowledge travels through the hallways, in low voices, with names attached.

46
00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:24.100
Without gossip, the newcomer would learn the group's invisible map over years, perhaps through bitter experience.

47
00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:27.750
And here an unsettling possibility emerges.

48
00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:32.360
What if gossip is not merely a flaw, as morality tells us?

49
00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:35.850
What if it is one of the things that make us human?

50
00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:42.220
That whisper that falls silent when you enter the room may be the echo of one of our species' oldest habits.

51
00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:49.390
One of the people who took this possibility most seriously was not a philosopher, but an anthropologist who watched monkeys.

52
00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:55.280
Early in his career, the British anthropologist Robin Dunbar spent years observing primates.

53
00:04:55.280 --> 00:05:01.330
What caught his attention was the amount of time monkeys and apes devote to grooming one another.

54
00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:09.300
A chimpanzee will part its companion's fur strand by strand, picking out parasites, patiently working away at it for hours.

55
00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:13.750
In some species, this takes up nearly twenty percent of the day.

56
00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:17.080
It has little to do with cleanliness alone.

57
00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:22.810
Grooming is a way of forming alliances, of affirming friendship, of saying "I'm on your side."

58
00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:29.180
Dunbar's question was a simple one: why don't humans pick through each other's hair?

59
00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:37.150
His answer appeared in a book he published in nineteen ninety-six, and its title said it all: Grooming, Gossip and the Evolution of Language.

60
00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:42.080
In his view, as groups grew larger, grooming began to fall short.

61
00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:47.170
Grooming is one-on-one; it can only be done with a single individual at a time.

62
00:05:47.170 --> 00:05:51.780
Talking, on the other hand, reaches several people at once.

63
00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:57.190
According to Dunbar, language developed, in a sense, as a kind of vocal grooming.

64
00:05:57.190 --> 00:06:05.960
And the real subject of this vocal grooming was other people: who gets along with whom, who can be trusted, who fails to keep their word.

65
00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:13.290
Dunbar and his team later listened in on people's conversations in cafés, on trains, and in university dining halls.

66
00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:25.260
The picture that emerged showed that roughly two-thirds of conversation time was devoted to social topics, that is, to people who were present or absent at that moment, to relationships, to personal experiences.

67
00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:31.150
Lofty subjects like politics, art, and science shared the small portion that remained.

68
00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:41.200
Dunbar's famous number grew out of this same line of thinking: he proposed that the number of people an individual can truly know and maintain a relationship with is about one hundred and fifty.

69
00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:45.330
And the way to bind that many people together was to talk about them.

70
00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:51.380
Seen from this angle, gossip is a whisper that holds the tribe together.

71
00:06:51.380 --> 00:06:59.190
In a hunter-gatherer community, one of the greatest dangers was the free rider, who took a share of the hunt and contributed nothing in return.

72
00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:02.360
There were no police, no courts.

73
00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:04.410
But there was reputation.

74
00:07:04.410 --> 00:07:07.580
And what carried reputation was gossip.

75
00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:15.390
Once the name of someone who always dodged their share began to come up around the fire, that person would either mend their ways or be left alone.

76
00:07:15.390 --> 00:07:18.400
Some took this idea into the laboratory.

77
00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:27.330
In twenty twelve, researchers in California, Matthew Feinberg and Robb Willer, had participants watch one player cheat the others in an economic game.

78
00:07:27.330 --> 00:07:31.140
Those who witnessed the unfairness saw their heart rates rise.

79
00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:35.590
Then some of them were given the chance to write a short note warning the next player.

80
00:07:35.590 --> 00:07:40.200
The heart rates of those who wrote the note slowed down, and they said they felt better.

81
00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:47.210
The researchers called this prosocial gossip: talking behind someone's back in order to protect someone else.

82
00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:51.820
The source of the relief was not malice, but a sense of justice.

83
00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:56.430
What's more, gossip is not as toxic as it is often thought to be.

84
00:07:56.430 --> 00:08:07.600
In twenty nineteen, Megan Robbins and Alexander Karan of the University of California fitted four hundred and sixty-seven people with small devices that captured brief recordings of ambient sound throughout the day.

85
00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:13.970
By their calculations, people spent an average of fifty-two minutes a day talking about someone who wasn't present.

86
00:08:13.970 --> 00:08:17.780
But roughly three-quarters of these conversations were neutral.

87
00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:23.190
Someone had started a new job, someone had moved, someone had had a baby.

88
00:08:23.190 --> 00:08:27.800
Disparaging gossip made up only a small part of the total.

89
00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:31.930
After such a defense, the objection is not long in coming.

90
00:08:31.930 --> 00:08:40.220
In the middle of the nineteenth century, in Copenhagen, a restless thinker named Søren Kierkegaard learned firsthand what idle talk can do.

91
00:08:40.220 --> 00:08:48.670
In eighteen forty-six, the city's satirical newspaper Corsaren, that is, The Corsair, made him a laughingstock for months on end.

92
00:08:48.670 --> 00:08:53.440
Its cartoons depicted Kierkegaard in trousers whose legs were of different lengths.

93
00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:58.690
In the street, children shouted after him, and people made fun of the way he walked.

94
00:08:58.690 --> 00:09:06.020
One of the country's most profound thinkers had been turned into a ridiculous figure in his own city by the gossip of a humor paper.

95
00:09:06.020 --> 00:09:14.790
In a long critical essay published that same year, known today as Two Ages, Kierkegaard gave a name to the sickness of his age: chatter.

96
00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:19.880
In his view, there is a tension between genuine speech and genuine silence.

97
00:09:19.880 --> 00:09:24.490
Only a person who can keep silent is able to say something of substance.

98
00:09:24.490 --> 00:09:28.620
Chatter, however, does away with this tension.

99
00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:33.230
No one truly says anything, yet everyone talks about everything.

100
00:09:33.230 --> 00:09:36.560
And the owner of this talk is never clear.

101
00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:42.290
Kierkegaard called it "the public": a faceless crowd in which no one takes responsibility.

102
00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:45.300
Gossip works in exactly this way.

103
00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:50.230
People say "everyone says so," but no one knows who that everyone is.

104
00:09:50.230 --> 00:09:56.120
Some eighty years later, Martin Heidegger carried the same intuition forward in his own language.

105
00:09:56.120 --> 00:10:05.690
In Being and Time, he described what he called "idle talk" as a way of speaking about a subject without understanding it, merely passing along what has been said about it.

106
00:10:05.690 --> 00:10:09.500
So who is right, Dunbar or Kierkegaard?

107
00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:11.550
Perhaps both.

108
00:10:11.550 --> 00:10:14.400
Gossip can hold the tribe together.

109
00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:20.450
But holding it together comes at a price, and that price is always paid by the one who isn't there.

110
00:10:20.450 --> 00:10:27.940
And there is a text that expressed this very price, centuries before the thinkers of the West, through a strikingly vivid comparison.

111
00:10:27.940 --> 00:10:34.150
The twelfth verse of the Surah Al-Hujurat in the Quran addresses believers with a series of warnings.

112
00:10:34.150 --> 00:10:38.760
It tells them to avoid much suspicion, for some suspicion is a sin.

113
00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:42.890
It asks them not to pry into one another's hidden faults.

114
00:10:42.890 --> 00:10:46.220
And then it commands them not to backbite one another.

115
00:10:46.220 --> 00:10:53.870
The question that follows is one of the harshest comparisons in Arabic literature: would any of you like to eat the flesh of his dead brother?

116
00:10:53.870 --> 00:10:57.360
And the answer comes at once: you would abhor it.

117
00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:02.770
This metaphor deserves a moment's attention, because every word in it was chosen deliberately.

118
00:11:02.770 --> 00:11:09.140
A brother, because the person being gossiped about is usually not a stranger but someone close.

119
00:11:09.140 --> 00:11:18.550
Dead, because the one spoken of behind their back is, in that moment, as defenseless as the dead; they cannot speak, cannot correct, cannot object.

120
00:11:18.550 --> 00:11:23.640
Flesh, because a person's reputation is as much a part of them as their own body.

121
00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:28.570
And eating, because gossip truly is a kind of consumption.

122
00:11:28.570 --> 00:11:32.860
The listener and the teller both feed on someone else's life.

123
00:11:32.860 --> 00:11:39.550
To that sweetness we spoke of in the first part, the verse assigns an entirely different taste: disgust.

124
00:11:39.550 --> 00:11:44.000
But the truly surprising part is a hadith recorded in Sahih Muslim.

125
00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:47.970
The Prophet Muhammad is asked what backbiting is.

126
00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:52.740
His answer is brief: it is mentioning your brother in a way he would dislike.

127
00:11:52.740 --> 00:11:57.830
The questioner objects: what if what I say is actually true of him?

128
00:11:57.830 --> 00:12:04.520
The reply stops one in their tracks: if what you say is true of him, you have backbitten him.

129
00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:07.690
And if it is not, you have slandered him.

130
00:12:07.690 --> 00:12:11.500
This is a distinction that runs against our intuitions.

131
00:12:11.500 --> 00:12:21.710
Most of us equate gossip with lying, and we defend ourselves by saying, "But it's true, I'm only telling the truth." In this tradition, however, truth is no excuse.

132
00:12:21.710 --> 00:12:25.360
Lying is already a separate, and graver, offense.

133
00:12:25.360 --> 00:12:33.010
Backbiting is precisely the act of saying what is true, in the wrong place, to the wrong people, in that person's absence.

134
00:12:33.010 --> 00:12:39.220
In the eleventh century, one of the thinkers who reflected most deeply on this question was Imam al-Ghazali.

135
00:12:39.220 --> 00:12:47.670
In his great work, the Ihya Ulum al-Din, the Revival of the Religious Sciences, he devoted an entire book to the afflictions of the tongue.

136
00:12:47.670 --> 00:12:54.680
Lying, mockery, idle argument, talebearing, and backbiting stood at the head of these afflictions.

137
00:12:54.680 --> 00:13:03.770
What set al-Ghazali apart was that he did not merely repeat the prohibition; he examined, one by one, the inner causes that drive people to backbite.

138
00:13:03.770 --> 00:13:08.380
The person who wants to vent their anger disparages their rival.

139
00:13:08.380 --> 00:13:13.630
The one who wants to fit in with a group sacrifices someone else to join in their conversation.

140
00:13:13.630 --> 00:13:17.760
The one who wants to exalt themselves rises by lowering another.

141
00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:21.570
The envious wear away the reputation of the person they envy.

142
00:13:21.570 --> 00:13:25.700
And the bored recount someone else's affairs simply for amusement.

143
00:13:25.700 --> 00:13:34.310
This list is strikingly close both to the envy measured a thousand years later in Japanese laboratories and to Dunbar's need for social bonding.

144
00:13:34.310 --> 00:13:39.400
Al-Ghazali does not deny these needs; he simply asks who pays the price.

145
00:13:39.400 --> 00:13:43.850
There is also a fair-minded side to al-Ghazali's approach.

146
00:13:43.850 --> 00:13:48.460
He accepts that in some situations backbiting may be necessary.

147
00:13:48.460 --> 00:13:52.910
Someone who has been wronged may describe their oppressor to a judge.

148
00:13:52.910 --> 00:13:56.880
A person may seek help from someone who can put an end to a wrongdoing.

149
00:13:56.880 --> 00:14:03.250
Someone about to marry or enter into a partnership may be told honestly about the true nature of the other party.

150
00:14:03.250 --> 00:14:09.460
By these standards, that warning note in Feinberg's experiment is not backbiting but a responsibility.

151
00:14:09.460 --> 00:14:16.950
What draws the line is intention and need: is this being said to protect someone, or merely for the pleasure of saying it?

152
00:14:16.950 --> 00:14:20.760
A similar sensitivity exists in other traditions as well.

153
00:14:20.760 --> 00:14:29.370
In Jewish thought, lashon hara, meaning evil speech, forbids saying hurtful things about another person, even when they are true.

154
00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:40.060
In eighteen seventy-three, the Lithuanian rabbi Yisrael Meir Kagan devoted an entire book to the subject, and it proved so influential that in time he came to be known by the book's title.

155
00:14:40.060 --> 00:14:48.510
It is remarkable that in different lands, in different languages, people arrived at the same conclusion: they knew that even the truth can wound.

156
00:14:48.510 --> 00:14:52.000
There is a quiet sorrow hidden within these texts.

157
00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:57.410
Because they all acknowledge the same thing: gossip comes very naturally to us.

158
00:14:57.410 --> 00:15:01.700
No one warns so insistently against something people do not do.

159
00:15:01.700 --> 00:15:07.910
As atonement for backbiting, al-Ghazali recommends asking forgiveness from the person who was spoken about.

160
00:15:07.910 --> 00:15:18.920
And this is perhaps the hardest part: going up to someone and saying, "I talked about you behind your back." Even imagining doing so shows us how far from innocent that conversation really was.

161
00:15:18.920 --> 00:15:21.610
So what should we do?

162
00:15:21.610 --> 00:15:24.140
Should we not speak at all?

163
00:15:24.140 --> 00:15:28.270
Is it possible, or even right, to bind the tongue completely?

164
00:15:28.270 --> 00:15:34.000
One of the oldest great works in the Turkish language answers this question with an unforgettable image.

165
00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:42.130
Between ten sixty-nine and ten seventy, Yusuf Khass Hajib of Balasagun presented a long work in verse to the Karakhanid ruler.

166
00:15:42.130 --> 00:15:47.060
It was called the Kutadgu Bilig, meaning the wisdom that brings happiness.

167
00:15:47.060 --> 00:15:55.990
The work touches on a great many subjects, from statecraft to ethics, from friendship to death, and one of its most remembered couplets is about the tongue.

168
00:15:55.990 --> 00:16:05.400
Yusuf Khass Hajib likens the tongue to a lion lying on the threshold of a door, and warns the master of the house: be careful, this lion will devour your head.

169
00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:10.810
At first glance, the image seems to be nothing more than a warning born of fear.

170
00:16:10.810 --> 00:16:16.220
But the lion's place matters: the threshold.

171
00:16:16.220 --> 00:16:20.830
Neither inside the house nor outside it, but exactly between the two.

172
00:16:20.830 --> 00:16:23.840
The tongue is the same.

173
00:16:23.840 --> 00:16:28.130
It waits at the door between the thoughts within us and the world outside.

174
00:16:28.130 --> 00:16:33.220
The lion is not an evil beast; it guards the house and frightens off strangers.

175
00:16:33.220 --> 00:16:37.670
But if it slips its chain, the first one it tears apart is its master.

176
00:16:37.670 --> 00:16:43.720
Yusuf Khass Hajib does not advise cutting out the tongue; he advises keeping it as a guardian.

177
00:16:43.720 --> 00:16:52.490
Elsewhere in the work, too, he praises those who speak little and to the point, and explains that when words are joined with knowledge, they elevate a person.

178
00:16:52.490 --> 00:16:58.380
On the other side of the East, Buddhism approaches the same question along a similar path.

179
00:16:58.380 --> 00:17:02.510
One of the steps on the Buddha's Noble Eightfold Path is right speech.

180
00:17:02.510 --> 00:17:11.280
It is described as refraining from four things: lying, divisive talk that sets people against one another, harsh words, and idle chatter.

181
00:17:11.280 --> 00:17:19.090
Buddhist texts tell us that the Buddha took care that what he said was true, that it was beneficial, and that it was spoken at the right time.

182
00:17:19.090 --> 00:17:24.180
A statement might be true, but if it is of no benefit, it is left unsaid.

183
00:17:24.180 --> 00:17:29.590
It might be true and beneficial, but if its moment has not yet come, one waits.

184
00:17:29.590 --> 00:17:39.480
This standard closely resembles the "three filters" story that circulates online, attributed to Socrates: is what you are about to say true, is it good, is it useful?

185
00:17:39.480 --> 00:17:43.450
Yet there is no trace of this story in any ancient source.

186
00:17:43.450 --> 00:17:51.900
And that is something that can't help but raise a small smile: the most widely shared advice against gossip travels around as a rumor of unknown origin.

187
00:17:51.900 --> 00:17:55.550
So is holding one's tongue always a virtue?

188
00:17:55.550 --> 00:17:58.080
Here we need to tread carefully.

189
00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:00.610
Silence has a dark side, too.

190
00:18:00.610 --> 00:18:10.180
If everyone in a workplace knows that someone is being systematically treated unjustly, yet no one speaks up, that silence is not a virtue; it is complicity.

191
00:18:10.180 --> 00:18:15.270
Al-Ghazali's exceptions and Feinberg's experiment point to exactly this.

192
00:18:15.270 --> 00:18:20.520
Sometimes talking about someone behind their back is the only way to protect another person.

193
00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:26.730
And the lion in the Kutadgu Bilig is not merely a sleeping animal; it is a guardian that roars when it must.

194
00:18:26.730 --> 00:18:34.380
The point is not to choose between silence and speech, but, in Kierkegaard's words, not to lose the tension between the two.

195
00:18:34.380 --> 00:18:40.430
Only someone who is able to stay silent can speak with weight when speaking becomes necessary.

196
00:18:40.430 --> 00:18:44.720
Today, the weight of these old questions has grown even heavier.

197
00:18:44.720 --> 00:18:50.290
Dunbar's circle of one hundred fifty people was the natural boundary of gossip around the fire.

198
00:18:50.290 --> 00:18:55.220
A rumor would travel as far as the edge of the village, and there it would die out.

199
00:18:55.220 --> 00:19:00.630
Now, a single screenshot in a messaging group can reach thousands of people within hours.

200
00:19:00.630 --> 00:19:07.480
The number of people who are not in the room has grown, too: now the ones being talked about are people we have never met and never will.

201
00:19:07.480 --> 00:19:15.610
Celebrities, ordinary people thrust into the spotlight by a single momentary mistake, strangers whose faces appear in a video.

202
00:19:15.610 --> 00:19:20.540
Kierkegaard's faceless public is rebuilt every day on the screens in our pockets.

203
00:19:20.540 --> 00:19:26.110
And what the caricatures of The Corsair once did, a few hundred shares can do today.

204
00:19:26.110 --> 00:19:31.040
That is why perhaps the oldest standard is also the most useful one.

205
00:19:31.040 --> 00:19:40.130
Hidden within the verse's image of the dead brother is a simple test: if that person were in this room right now, would I phrase this sentence the same way?

206
00:19:40.130 --> 00:19:44.900
If the answer is yes, I am probably making an honest assessment.

207
00:19:44.900 --> 00:19:51.430
But if my voice is dropping and I find myself glancing at the door, then the lion at the threshold is about to wake.

208
00:19:51.430 --> 00:19:54.920
It is no coincidence that gossip tastes sweet.

209
00:19:54.920 --> 00:20:02.570
This is the taste of an ancient need, one that binds us to one another, teaches us whom to trust, and holds the tribe together.

210
00:20:02.570 --> 00:20:07.180
But like every sweet thing, it makes us sick when taken beyond measure.

211
00:20:07.180 --> 00:20:18.350
That whisper that falls silent the moment you walk into the room reminds us of two things at once: that people cannot live without talking about one another, and that the person being talked about will, one day,

212
00:20:18.350 --> 00:20:20.560
surely walk into that room.

213
00:20:20.560 --> 00:20:24.690
We have come to the end of today's episode of Nöron Felsefe.

214
00:20:24.690 --> 00:20:27.380
Until we meet again in a new story.
